    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d834">

                <pb facs="0839" n="721" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg721"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811f.244/98: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Panama</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >South</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-05-13T12:00:00-04:00">May 13,
                    1929—noon</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>33. Your despatch No. 1998, of April 22. Please address a note to the Panaman
                    Government referring further to its note of April 11 and informing the
                    Government that you have been instructed to point out that there is nothing in
                    treaties now in force to prevent the United States Government from making sales
                    at the Canal Commissaries to anyone to whom it may wish to extend the privilege
                    of purchasing there. Since the abrogation of the Taft Agreement<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d834-f1"/> sales have been restricted in practice to certain classes
                    of persons by the voluntary action of this Government and as a matter of policy,
                    but changes in the present policy or the authorization of particular sales in
                    special cases are questions entirely within the discretion of the United States
                    Government so long as the treaty signed July 28, 1926,<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d834-f2"/> remains unratified.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
